  "diagnosis_text": "I confirmed a severe, direction-specific problem affecting **h1 \u2192 h5**.\n\n- **Path:** `h1 \u2014 s4 \u2014 s2 \u2014 s1 \u2014 s3 \u2014 s6 \u2014 h5`\n- All links on that path are administratively up/active.\n- Ping succeeds with no loss, but latency is very unstable and high: **56\u2013313 ms**, **121 ms average**.\n- A 5-second TCP test from h1 to h5 delivered effectively no useful data:\n  - sender accepted only **131 KiB** at about **0.21 Mbit/s**\n  - receiver reported **0 bytes**\n- The reverse direction, `h5 \u2192 h1`, achieved about **19.6 Mbit/s** at the receiver. This makes a general endpoint failure or whole-path outage unlikely.\n\nThe strongest local evidence is on **s2 port 1, toward s1**\u2014the forward-path egress:\n\n- Queue backlog was present: **12,112 bytes / 8 packets**\n- **378 queue-overlimit events in two seconds**\n- No interface errors or OpenFlow port drops were reported.\n\nOther forward links sampled did not show backlog or overlimits. Thus the problem is localized to the **outbound s2:1 \u2192 s1:1 direction**, most likely queue congestion or an egress traffic-control/rate-limit condition there. The link is up, so this is not a link-down fault.\n\nNext operational check: inspect the OVS/qdisc or shaping policy and any competing traffic affecting **s2 port 1**.",
